      "title": "bet365 cuts 340 roles across European hubs, about 3% of its workforce, on the day it launches in West Virginia and Washington DC",
      "text": "bet365 confirmed on 8 September that it is cutting a reported 340 employees, most based in England with additional reductions in Gibraltar and Malta. In a statement to the Racing Post the operator said: 'As an international business, we continually review and assess our operations to ensure the business' long-term future. We're currently facing a highly competitive trading environment, plus increased regulatory and tax-related [pressures]', confirming the reduction of '340 roles across our European hubs, which is the equivalent of around three per cent of the workforce'. A voluntary buyout programme will run before any compulsory redundancies, so the final figure may land lower. The cuts are directly linked to the UK nearly doubling remote gaming duty from 21% to 40% in April 2026; per the Betting and Gaming Council, close to 4,500 jobs have now gone across the sector. The timing is the story. The same day the redundancies were confirmed, bet365 went live in West Virginia and Washington DC, taking it to 19 US jurisdictions for online sports betting and, where available, iGaming, under its 'Winning Is Everything' campaign. Company disclosures show new-market licences secured in Brazil, Peru, Serbia and further US states. So this is not a business in retreat but one reallocating headcount out of a high-tax mature market and into launch capacity elsewhere, which is precisely the pattern that determines B2B demand. Operators funding multi-market expansion while cutting European staff cannot also grow in-house platform, compliance and localisation teams proportionally, and the make-or-buy calculation on market entry technology shifts accordingly. For anyone selling PAM, payments or regulatory-reporting capability, the addressable question is no longer whether tier-one operators will expand, but which parts of the launch stack they will stop building themselves.",
