      "title": "UK Chancellor weighs doubling Machine Games Duty, with industry analysts warning of 3,000 shop closures and a GBP 70m racing levy hit",
      "text": "The Times reports that Chancellor John Healey is considering an increase to Machine Games Duty ahead of the UK Autumn Budget, as the government seeks funding for defence spending and cost-of-living relief. A doubling would take the lower rate from 5% to 10%, the standard rate from 20% to 40% and the higher rate from 25% to 50%. Industry analysts told the Racing Post such a move could trigger nearly 3,000 betting shop closures and reduce the horse racing levy by around GBP 70m. A BGC spokesperson said it 'would put further pressure on betting shops, casinos and other venues, cost jobs and investment, weaken high streets and benefit the growing illegal gambling market', adding that 'by the end of 2026, more than 600 betting shops will have closed and 5,000 jobs will have been lost since last year's budget following increases in remote gaming duty'. The measure would stack on top of Rachel Reeves' near-doubling of Remote Gaming Duty from 21% to 40% implemented in April 2026, and a General Betting Duty rise from 15% to 25% scheduled for April 2027. Gordon Brown has separately called for a Machine Games Duty hike to fund winter fuel relief. The cumulative picture matters more than any single rate: the UK is now on a three-year path where every major duty line rises, and interim results at Entain, evoke, Flutter and FDJ United already show the first-order effects. For platform and product teams the consequence is a reordering of the roadmap around margin rather than growth. When gross margin per player falls by a third, the investment case shifts from acquisition volume to bonus efficiency, cost-to-serve, retention economics and automation of anything currently done by headcount, and omnichannel becomes a question about which retail estate survives.",
