    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "FTSE Russell confirms Entain's relegation to the FTSE 250 on 21 September after a six-year run on the premium index",
    "meta": "SBC News | 3 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "FTSE Russell confirmed in its September 2026 index review that Entain will be deleted from the FTSE 100 at the close on 18 September and enter the FTSE 250 on 21 September, alongside housebuilder Persimmon, with Ithaca Energy and EasyJet replacing them. Entain joined the FTSE 100 in June 2020, saw its stock double from roughly GBP 7.60 to GBP 18 within a year and peak at GBP 21.56 in October 2021; it now sits at GBP 5.23, a fall of more than 75%. Contributing factors include UK tax rises and BetMGM's late-July guidance that full-year net revenue and adjusted EBITDA will land at the lower end of ranges.",
    "why": "Index deletion is not merely reputational, because it forces mechanical selling by tracker funds and narrows the investor base, which raises the cost of equity precisely when the same company is arguing it can consolidate a taxed market. That combination shapes supplier negotiations directly: Entain's stated strategy is to take share from mid-tier operators struggling with the RGD rise from 21% to 40%, and executing that with a higher cost of capital means the capital-light route wins. In practice that favours buying platform, content and risk capability over building it, but with harder commercial terms and more scrutiny of total cost of ownership. The corporate tax charge rising to GBP 57.8m from GBP 19.5m is the concrete number to hold onto, because it quantifies how much of the H1 growth was consumed by duty rather than reinvested, and it explains why efficiency arguments now travel further than growth arguments.",
    "expanded": "SBC reports the Ladbrokes Coral owner's market capitalisation is now the lowest on the index, giving the figure as GBP 3.31m, which is self-evidently a typographical error for GBP 3.31bn. Despite the share price fall, Entain beat H1 expectations, with net gaming revenue up 5% to GBP 2.55bn helped by the early stages of the 2026 World Cup. CFO Michael Snape said the business is taking market share as mid-tier operators struggle with the Remote Gaming Duty rise from 21% to 40%, a change that pushed Entain's corporate tax charge to GBP 57.8m from GBP 19.5m. FY25 net loss widened to GBP 681m. The BetMGM guidance downgrade in late July removed one of the equity story's clearest growth props, since the US joint venture had been the principal argument for a premium multiple. Entain's relegation also lands in the same fortnight as bet365's 340 European redundancies and Flutter's Paddy Power estate review, which together make the point that the UK duty changes are now visible in headcount, estate and index composition rather than only in guidance language. The deletion takes effect two days before markets open on 21 September.",
    "source": "https://sbcnews.co.uk/sportsbook/2026/09/03/entain-to-leave-ftse-100/",
