    "title": "Veikkaus returns to growth as it prepares to pay about EUR 1bn for a 10-year exclusive Finnish lottery and slots licence",
    "meta": "SBC News | 3 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Finland's outgoing state monopoly Veikkaus Group returned to growth in H1 2026 ahead of the licensed market opening on 1 July 2027. Veikkaus will lose exclusivity over betting and online casino but retains exclusive rights to lottery, slot machines, scratch cards and physical table games, at a market-based compensation fee of approximately EUR 1bn payable across the decade-long licence, with a significant front-loaded portion expected due by the end of this year. Actual sales revenue rose from EUR 466.4m to EUR 471.3m and net profit climbed from EUR 229.6m to EUR 234.2m. Digital channels generated 64.5% of GGR, up 3.6% year on year, with 2,672,000 registered accounts.",
    "why": "Finland is the last significant Nordic monopoly to open, and the structure of this deal defines the competitive shape new entrants will meet from July 2027. Veikkaus keeps the highest-frequency, lowest-acquisition-cost products, lottery and slots, and pays for that privilege up front, which means it will defend those verticals hard and cross-sell from a 2.67 million-account base that no entrant can match at launch. Anyone modelling Finnish entry should therefore assume the addressable pool is betting and online casino only, against an incumbent with national brand recall and a retail network. The more interesting signal for B2B is Fennica Gaming, Veikkaus' B2B arm, growing revenue 80.6% to EUR 10.3m across 21 markets and three continents: a state monopoly is monetising its own platform and content externally, which adds a well-capitalised competitor in exactly the lottery-adjacent and content-supply niches where localisation expertise is the differentiator.",
    "expanded": "For the six months to 30 June, digital channels generated 64.5% of GGR, up 3.6% year on year, with 35.5% from physical points of sale. Lottery and Land Based Gaming GGR was EUR 317.9m against EUR 317.5m in H1 2025, with lottery-focused digital GGR up 9.1%. Betting and iCasino GGR rose to EUR 151.6m from EUR 146.5m, with digital-only GGR up 5%. B2B subsidiary Fennica Gaming grew revenue 80.6% to EUR 10.3m from EUR 5.7m, driven by activations in Canada and Italy plus expansion across Germany, Mexico, the Czech Republic and Iceland; Fennica now delivers games across three continents and 21 markets, supporting Veikkaus' stated ambition to become a competitive international gambling company by the end of 2030. The EUR 1bn compensation fee is calculated on a market basis for the exclusive rights Veikkaus retains, and the front-loaded portion falling due before the end of 2026 means the group is funding a substantial payment before the competitive market even opens. Finland's model, splitting exclusivity by vertical rather than ending it outright, is a template other monopoly markets are likely to study, since it preserves state revenue from the highest-yield products while satisfying pressure to open the contested ones.",
