    "summary": "Super Technologies, formerly Superbet Group, asserted its 'right to protect its employees and properties', saying it will retain staff and repurpose venues hit by municipal gambling bans rather than close them and make redundancies. Emergency Ordinance OUG 7/2026, signed on 24 February by Prime Minister Ilie Bolojan, transferred land-based venue licensing from the National Gambling Office (ONJN) to municipal councils and mayors, allowing them to set zones and annual local authorisation fees. Slatina, Bacau, Sibiu, Braila, Iasi and Zalau have adopted prohibitions, with venue limits in Bucharest's zone six. Affected venues become 'Home of Superliga' community centres where supporters watch fixtures, with no one-to-one betting.",
    "why": "Devolving venue licensing to hundreds of municipalities converts a single national compliance rule into a patchwork that changes with local election cycles, and Superbet's own complaint that 'restrictions have not been applied uniformly' is the operative problem. Any retail or omnichannel system now needs venue-level rule configuration rather than country-level, covering permitted activity, local fee schedules and differing exemptions, and it needs to change without a release cycle because a council vote can take effect faster than a quarterly deployment. The Lottery vendor exemption is worth noting, because unequal treatment between private operators and the state lottery in the same street is the kind of asymmetry that drives litigation and eventual redrafting. Romania is also compounding this with tax, having raised the online licence levy from 21% to 27% on 1 August 2026, so operators are absorbing a rate rise and a retail disruption simultaneously.",
    "expanded": "Andrei Popa, General Manager of Super Romania, said: 'An administrative decision can change what we are allowed to do in a space, but it cannot cancel the things that unite us... For us, this means being part of a community: staying there even when it is harder.' Superbet says restrictions 'have not been applied uniformly', with varying exemptions and no limits on Lottery vendors. The group runs more than 1,000 agencies and reported over 2,600 employees in its 2024 accounts. Founded in Bucharest in 2008 by Sacha Dragic, it is backed by a EUR 1.3bn financing package agreed in 2025 and led by Blackstone and HPS Investment Partners. Romania's online licence tax rose from 21% to 27% on 1 August 2026. ONJN President Vlad-Cristian Soare was dismissed on 21 August and replaced by Valentin-Ioan Tomescu, adding regulator instability to the licensing fragmentation. The 'Home of Superliga' conversion is a notable defensive play: by keeping the lease, the staff and the footfall while removing the regulated activity, Superbet preserves the option to restore betting if a council reverses course or courts strike down the ordinance, and retains a branded physical presence that continues to acquire online customers.",
