    "section": "North America",
    "title": "Tenth Circuit denies Kalshi an injunction, leaving it exposed to enforcement under Utah's gambling laws",
    "meta": "SBC Americas | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "The Tenth Circuit Court of Appeals on 8 September denied Kalshi's request to block Utah from enforcing its gambling laws while the operator appeals a District Court loss. The panel found Kalshi met none of the four factors needed to justify an emergency injunction pending appeal: 'Kalshi has not shown these factors weigh in its favor. Accordingly, we deny its motion.' The District Court had earlier denied the injunction and additionally granted summary judgment to the state. Kalshi has parallel appeals pending in the Second Circuit, covering New York and Connecticut, and the Sixth Circuit, covering Ohio, and separately lost an Iowa injunction on 9 September.",
    "why": "The significance is the accumulation rather than any single ruling. Kalshi's legal position depends on federal preemption applying uniformly, and a growing set of appellate refusals makes state-by-state operation the practical reality regardless of the eventual constitutional answer. That has a direct architectural consequence for anyone building event-contract or exchange-style products: the system has to support jurisdictional availability at the contract-category level, with different age thresholds, different permitted underlyings and the ability to withdraw a market in one state while keeping it live in another. Judge Shelby's observation that 'Kalshi concedes gambling is a field that has been traditionally regulated by the states' is the doctrinal hinge, and it points towards the compliance burden looking like sports betting's rather than a derivatives venue's. Product teams treating prediction markets as a way around state licensing should read the Utah outcome as pricing that assumption.",
    "expanded": "Judge Shelby wrote that 'Kalshi concedes gambling is a field that has been \"traditionally regulated by the states,\" not the federal government', citing the Supreme Court's statement that 'the Government has a substantial interest in supporting the policy of [non-gambling] States'. Utah is described as the most legislatively gambling-averse state in the country, with no commercial gambling offering at all; HB 243, aimed at Kalshi, Polymarket and others, took effect in May 2026. Governor Spencer Cox escalated the dispute earlier this year by quote-tweeting CFTC Chair Michael Selig. The Utah denial sits alongside the Michigan preliminary injunction with its USD 500,000 daily geofencing penalty, the Ninth Circuit's ruling for Nevada, and New York's pending SDNY case where the state has asked the court to give the CFTC's emergency order no weight. Against that, the Third Circuit ruled 2-1 for Kalshi on New Jersey in April, creating the circuit split that makes Supreme Court review increasingly likely. For now the operating picture is a company running a federally licensed venue while being enjoined, state by state, from offering its highest-volume product category.",
    "source": "https://sbcamericas.com/2026/09/08/kalshi-denied-tenth-circuit-appeal/",
    "tags": ["Kalshi", "Utah", "Prediction markets", "Courts"]
