    "section": "North America",
    "title": "Underdog surrenders daily fantasy licences in seven states to protect its CFTC-regulated prediction markets business",
    "meta": "SBC News | 7 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Underdog will voluntarily surrender its fantasy sports licences in Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, Mississippi, New Jersey, Ohio and Pennsylvania after those states determined that operating a prediction markets platform alongside fantasy sports is incompatible. Founder and CEO Jeremy Levine announced on X on 5 September: 'Right after kickoff on Wednesday, we will be shutting down Drafts in seven states: MA, MD, MI, MS, NJ, PA and OH.' The Drafts product became unavailable from 10 September. Levine said: 'Those states have taken a legal viewpoint we disagree with: if we offer our CFTC-licensed products we cannot offer fantasy sports in those states. So we had to choose.' Draft contests remain live in 33 states and Canada outside Ontario.",
    "why": "This is the first clean data point on what a state-licensed operator will give up to keep an exchange product, and Underdog chose the exchange. That tells you the internal economics: Levine noted Drafts 'don't get anywhere near the usage of our other offerings', so the surrendered licences were cheap relative to CFTC-regulated volume. The structural lesson is that regulators are treating the two models as mutually exclusive within a single corporate entity, which means multi-product operators may face a licensing fork rather than a portfolio decision, and any platform serving both needs genuine entity-level and product-level separation of licences, wallets and player records. The IG Group acquisition context sharpens it further: a FTSE 100 fintech paying USD 1.1bn for an asset whose regulatory permissions are being actively narrowed by seven states is a live case study in how prediction-market risk is being underwritten.",
    "expanded": "Levine added that Drafts 'don't get anywhere near the usage of our other offerings' and that the company has 'some ideas about how to get Drafts back to more customers'. Already-entered drafts were allowed to continue as normal, but customers in the seven states could not enter new ones. Underdog agreed in late July to a USD 1.1bn acquisition by FTSE 100 fintech IG Group; over 60% of Underdog's monthly actives are under 30, against an IG average customer age of 42, which was a stated part of the strategic rationale. The seven states involved include several of the largest regulated fantasy and sports betting markets in the country, so the decision is not a rounding error in addressable population, only in Underdog's own product mix. It also sets a precedent other operators will be asked to follow: if a regulator can condition a fantasy licence on abstaining from CFTC-registered products, the same logic extends naturally to sportsbook and casino licences, which would make the choice considerably more expensive for a company like FanDuel or DraftKings than it was for Underdog.",
