    "section": "North America",
    "title": "Blumenthal, Tonko and Foushee accuse FanDuel of 'misleading and incomplete' answers on its VIP programme",
    "meta": "SBC Americas | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "In a letter sent on 3 September, Senator Richard Blumenthal and Representatives Paul Tonko and Valerie Foushee accused FanDuel of 'misleading and incomplete responses regarding its exploitative VIP service'. The legislators wrote: 'FanDuel is making an active effort to protect bettors from harmful gambling and instead appears to be promoting addiction... We demand real answers to our previous letter.' The trio first wrote to FanDuel, MLB and the MLBPA on 10 August over a Cameo-recorded video from Phillies star Bryce Harper that a FanDuel staffer sent to a user with a known gambling addiction. FanDuel replied on 14 August focusing on responsible gambling investments and monitoring tools, and offered no new comment.",
    "why": "The specific accusation is that FanDuel 'failed to answer our questions about the specific steps taken to vet and monitor VIP activities', and that is a documentation question before it is a policy question. The legislators are asking whether promotions are targeted at bettors on losing streaks or at those reducing activity, which requires an operator to be able to reconstruct, per intervention, what risk signals were present when a VIP action was taken and who authorised it. Most VIP tooling is built to enable outreach, not to evidence its appropriateness after the fact, and that asymmetry is exactly what turns a single Cameo video into a congressional exchange. The second thread is equally pointed: the letter alleges FanDuel Predicts 'undermines the integrity of, if not bypasses' state regulation because it is offered to over-18s in states where FanDuel Sportsbook is unavailable, tying the VIP question to the prediction-market perimeter.",
    "expanded": "Harper said he did not know the recording would be used as a VIP reward. Congress reiterated all eight of its original questions, including whether FanDuel targets promotions at bettors on losing streaks or those cutting back, and whether it will suspend the VIP programme. FanDuel referred back to its 14 August letter rather than offering new comment. The VIP or host model has been the single most scrutinised area of US sports betting operations, because it concentrates a large share of revenue in a small number of high-spending accounts and creates a direct commercial incentive for individual staff to sustain that spend. What makes this exchange consequential is that it moves the argument from whether VIP programmes are risky in principle to whether a specific operator can produce records showing its own controls functioned, and the legislators have now twice found the answers inadequate. The FanDuel Predicts allegation compounds the exposure by connecting VIP conduct to the age and licensing asymmetry between event contracts and sportsbook, which is the same asymmetry Michigan's court cited when it noted Kalshi admits users at 18 against a state minimum of 21.",
