    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "ANJL takes Minas Gerais betting advertising decree to Brazil's Supreme Court in ADI 8014 over federal competence",
    "meta": "iGaming Brazil | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "The Associacao Nacional de Jogos e Loterias has filed a direct action of unconstitutionality at Brazil's Supreme Federal Court against a Minas Gerais state decree restricting advertising, publicity and promotional activity by fixed-odds betting companies on state public property and in public spaces. Per the STF's case system the action is ADI 8014, assigned to Justice Flavio Dino. ANJL argues the state measure imposes restrictions exceeding states' constitutional competence and interferes in a matter already governed at federal level, generating legal uncertainty and differentiated regulatory treatment for a sector subject to national rules.",
    "why": "The federal-versus-state advertising fight is the single biggest source of operational unpredictability in Brazil right now, because a nationally licensed operator can face materially different marketing rules in each of 27 federative units. That is a campaign-management and creative-approval problem before it is a legal one: it requires geo-targeted creative rules, state-level approval workflows and the ability to suppress specific placements by jurisdiction. The Procuradoria-Geral da Republica backing a parallel challenge to Rio Grande do Sul's Law no 16,508/2026 is the encouraging signal, since federal prosecutors arguing for suspension on competence grounds makes an eventual pro-uniformity ruling more likely. But until the STF rules, the prudent build is to assume fragmentation persists, and the cost of that assumption falls on marketing operations and affiliate compliance rather than on the platform core.",
    "expanded": "The filing lands amid a widening federal-versus-state dispute over how far Brazilian states and municipalities can regulate betting promotion, following moves such as Rio de Janeiro's Decree 58.274/2026. In a parallel case concerning Rio Grande do Sul, the Procuradoria-Geral da Republica has backed a challenge to state Law no 16,508/2026, which set rules for fixed-odds betting advertising, publicity and sponsorship, arguing for suspension of the norm and stressing the constitutional division of powers between the Union and the states. ANJL says the Minas Gerais case rests on the same principle: preserving federal competence over the licensed betting market and guaranteeing uniformity, predictability and legal certainty for authorised operators nationwide. Justice Flavio Dino's assignment is notable given his prior role as justice minister, when he was directly involved in federal enforcement against illegal betting. The broader context is that state-level restrictions have proliferated precisely because federal advertising rules are seen by some legislators as insufficient, and the Senate has separately approved restrictions on advertising and sponsorship at national level, so a ruling on competence will not settle the substantive question of how tightly betting promotion is ultimately constrained in Brazil.",
