    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "Brazil's centralised self-exclusion platform passes 1.2 million registrations in eight months, 3% of active Sigap bettors",
    "meta": "Yogonet | 7 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Data released by Brazil's Secretariat of Prizes and Betting shows more than 1.2 million people had voluntarily requested exclusion from authorised fixed-odds betting platforms as of September 2026, roughly eight months after the Centralized Self-Exclusion Platform launched in December 2025. The total equals 3% of the 40 million active CPF registrations in the Betting Management System, Sigap. A BNLData comparison of official data from seven countries found Brazil's absolute volume now exceeds virtually all major national schemes; the UK's GAMSTOP took eight years to reach 614,738 accumulated registrations.",
    "why": "A national self-exclusion register at this scale is an operational integration problem with no margin for error, because every one of 1.2 million CPFs must be checked at registration, login, deposit and marketing selection across 85 authorised operators, in real time, with the register still growing. The interesting question is what drove adoption so fast: a centralised, state-run, single-request mechanism covering all licensed platforms removes the friction that keeps operator-level schemes small, and that design choice is now the benchmark other regulators will cite. Yogonet's caution about comparability is important and worth carrying into any internal analysis, since Brazil's 3% is measured against active registered bettors while UK, Swedish and Dutch figures are typically expressed against total adult population, and counting rules differ, with Germany's OASIS permitting repeat registrations. The number is a signal about platform design, not proof of exceptional harm.",
    "expanded": "Brazil achieved the figure in a market regulated for just over 18 months, with Law 14.790/2023 taking effect in January 2025. Yogonet cautions the numbers do not prove Brazil has proportionally higher gambling harm: Brazil's 3% is calculated against active registered bettors, whereas figures published in the UK, Sweden and the Netherlands are typically based on total adult population. Counting methods also differ, with Germany's OASIS permitting the same person to self-exclude repeatedly, and scope varies, with Spain's RGIAJ covering only state-regulated online gambling while the Brazilian and Australian registers cover all nationally licensed platforms. The comparison with GAMSTOP is nonetheless striking on absolute volume and on time-to-scale, and it reflects a structural difference: Brazilian self-exclusion is tied to the CPF, the universal taxpayer identifier already required for account opening, so identity resolution is unambiguous in a way it is not in jurisdictions relying on name, address and date of birth matching. That makes the Brazilian register both easier to enforce and harder to circumvent, and it is the clearest example yet of a national identity number turning a player-protection policy into a reliable technical control.",
    "source": "https://www.yogonet.com/international/news/2026/09/07/126268-brazil-selfexclusion-registrations-top-12-million-eight-months-after-national-platform-launch",
