    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "Chile's tax authority defends VAT on offshore betting platforms, projecting a first quarterly payment above USD 100m in October",
    "meta": "Emol / El Mercurio | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "SII director Jorge Trujillo told Chile's Senate Economy Commission on 8 September that Resolution No 69 of 2026 created a mechanism for foreign betting, casino and gaming platforms to declare and pay tax in Chile, and that once registered they must settle VAT owed for the last 36 tax periods. Fiscalisation deputy director Carolina Saravia said 39 platforms are registered, all on quarterly payment, so the first payment falls due in October 2026, with an estimate 'slightly above USD 100 million'. A further ten platforms that did not register voluntarily were folded into a change-of-taxpayer mechanism under Resolucion Exenta No 94, published 15 July, shifting liability to withholding agents.",
    "why": "Chile is now doing something genuinely unusual: taxing an activity it has not legalised, while simultaneously blocking it. Trujillo was explicit that 'el habilitar una plataforma para el pago de los impuestos adecuados no valida en caso alguno la ejecucion de este tipo de actividades', and he acknowledged that if site blocking succeeds the taxable base disappears. That contradiction is the risk to model for anyone contemplating Chilean entry, because retroactive VAT across 36 periods is a material historic liability that crystallises on registration, and the withholding-agent mechanism means non-registration does not avoid it. Senator Gaston Saavedra's objection that 'no basta con recaudar' captures the political instability of the arrangement: revenue collection creates a fiscal constituency for tolerating the grey market, which cuts directly against the licensing bill the industry has been waiting years for. Tax registration is not a step towards a licence here.",
    "expanded": "Saravia said: 'La estimacion que nosotros tenemos para este primer pago es un poco superior a US$100 millones.' Trujillo stressed that taxation does not legalise the activity. Commission chair Gaston Saavedra of the Socialist Party objected: 'Lo peligroso es que el SII valida una actividad que es ilicita... no basta con recaudar.' The SII grounds the charge in article 8 letter n) of DL 825, covering digital entertainment. Trujillo said that if site blocking succeeds, the taxable base disappears. That last point connects this directly to the recent Subtel-ordered blocking of dozens of betting domains following judicial rulings, and to industry associations' complaints that the blocking exceeded the court order. Chile therefore has three arms of the state pulling in different directions: courts ordering blocks, the telecoms regulator implementing them, and the tax authority registering the same operators and collecting retroactive VAT from them. The USD 100m October figure will make the fiscal argument concrete for the first time, and it arrives while Congress is still considering the online gambling licensing framework that would resolve the underlying legal status.",
