    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "Mexico's Segob completes draft reform of the 1947 Federal Gaming and Sweepstakes Law and sends it to the Security Cabinet",
    "meta": "Yogonet | 7 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Mexico's Secretariat of the Interior has completed a proposal to reform the Federal Gaming and Sweepstakes Law, targeting money laundering and tax fraud at casinos and betting establishments. Segob head Rosa Icela Rodriguez said the internal review of the framework, which dates from 1947, is finished, and the initiative will go to the Security Cabinet before any formal presentation. Speaking at the La Mananera del Pueblo press conference, she said she had met casino and betting operators and warned them against corrupting inspectors. Rodriguez said around 20 casinos linked to money laundering or tax fraud have been closed.",
    "why": "Mexico is one of the largest under-regulated gambling markets in the hemisphere, operating on a statute written before commercial television, and the framing of this reform matters more than its existence. This is being routed through the Security Cabinet as an anti-money-laundering and enforcement measure, not through economic policy as a licensing modernisation, which suggests the first draft will emphasise AML obligations, beneficial ownership, source-of-funds and inspection powers rather than a clear online authorisation pathway. For platform and payments providers that points to KYC, transaction monitoring and reporting requirements arriving before commercial clarity does. The precedent is not encouraging on timing: the Chamber of Deputies approved a similar bill in December 2014 and it died in the Senate. Until draft text is published there is no basis for planning specifics, but the direction of travel favours compliance capability over market-entry capability.",
    "expanded": "Rodriguez told operators: 'First of all, what I want from you is that, please, do not corrupt personnel from the Secretariat of the Interior, because I will close your businesses and I will put any colleagues who are receiving money from you in jail.' In exchange she offered to regularise pending injunctions and procedures, saying the agreement had held across her two years in office. She stressed that the roughly 20 closures resulted from criminal and tax investigations run with the Attorney General's Office and the Financial Intelligence Unit rather than from Segob's administrative role, and instructed both operators and inspectors to film inspections so 'both sides will have evidence of any issue'. Separate reporting put the number of publicly confirmed closures at 13 against around 20 establishments identified as having problems. No draft text or detailed measures have been published and no timetable has been given for Security Cabinet consideration or submission to Congress. The instruction to record inspections is a small but telling detail: it addresses a governance problem, inspector corruption, that any new licensing regime would otherwise inherit, and it signals that Segob sees process integrity as a precondition for the reform rather than a consequence of it.",
    "source": "https://www.yogonet.com/international/news/2026/09/07/126274-mexico-segob-advances-new-gaming-and-sweepstakes-law-sends-proposal-to-security-cabinet",
