    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "Buenos Aires Province begins collecting a 2% levy on online slot prizes, targeting ARS 35-40bn extra revenue a month",
    "meta": "G3 Newswire | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Buenos Aires Province has begun applying a 2% contribution to prizes generated by online games of immediate resolution, including virtual slots. The measure was formalised through Resolution 1149/2026 of the Provincial Institute of Lotteries and Casinos, signed on 1 September, with collection beginning on 8 September. It applies to the province's seven licensed online platforms: Bet365, Betano, Betsson, BetWarrior, BPlay, Sportsbet and Stake. Sports betting is outside its scope. Players are the taxpayers, with operators acting as substitute withholding agents required to file weekly sworn declarations and deposit withheld amounts monthly.",
    "why": "A levy on prizes rather than on GGR or turnover is a distinct engineering problem, because it has to be withheld at the moment of a winning outcome, per qualifying game type, with the player as taxpayer and the operator as substitute agent. That means wallet and game-integration changes: identifying immediate-resolution games separately from sports betting, applying withholding at payout, exposing the deduction to the player transparently, and producing weekly sworn declarations of qualifying prizes with monthly settlement. Getting the game-type classification wrong creates either under-withholding liability or incorrect deductions from winning players, and both are hard to unwind. The province is simultaneously enforcing hard, having blocked 537 unauthorised gambling websites following more than 800 complaints, and requiring biometric identification for access to licensed platforms, so Buenos Aires is worth studying as a template for provincial-level tax and identity requirements stacking on a single licence.",
    "expanded": "The levy is not new in principle: Article 137 of provincial Law 15.079, promulgated in December 2018, established a contribution of between 1% and 3% on all prizes from successive bets on immediate-resolution games, and Resolution 1149/2026 sets the rate at 2%. Proceeds go first to municipalities under the coparticipation formula in Decree 356/04, with the remainder to the Fondo de Progreso e Inclusion Social, funding health, education and social development. Buenos Aires government minister Carlos Bianco said the province expects ARS 35bn to ARS 40bn in additional monthly revenue. The province simultaneously highlighted more than 800 complaints against unlicensed platforms, the most recent leading to the blocking of 537 unauthorised gambling websites, plus its biometric identification requirement for licensed platform access. Argentina's province-by-province licensing model already forces operators to hold separate authorisations with separate technical requirements, and the addition of a prize-level withholding obligation in the largest province increases the fixed cost of maintaining that footprint. The exclusion of sports betting from scope is significant for product mix, since it makes online casino measurably less attractive on a net-to-player basis within the province.",
