    "section": "Africa",
    "title": "Africa's first continent-wide Safer Gambling Week opens with six regulators behind the #PlaySafeAfrica campaign",
    "meta": "Focus Gaming News Africa | 7 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Africa's inaugural continent-wide Safer Gambling Week began on Monday 7 September and runs to Friday 11 September, organised by the African iGaming Alliance under the #PlaySafeAfrica campaign. Each of the five days targets a different theme: responsible gambling, understanding personal limits, using safer-gambling tools, preventing underage gambling, and the risks of illegal and unlicensed operators. Supporting bodies include the Lagos State Lotteries and Gaming Authority, South Africa's National Gambling Board, Uganda's National Lotteries and Gaming Regulatory Board, the Gaming Board of Tanzania, the Malawi Gaming and Lotteries Authority and the Association of Gaming Operators Kenya.",
    "why": "Coordinated multi-regulator campaigns are usually the precursor to coordinated regulatory expectations, and the themes chosen tell you what those expectations will cover: limit-setting, safer-gambling tool availability, age verification and illegal-market messaging. All four are product requirements before they are communications requirements, and in African markets the hard part is delivering them on the actual access channels, which are heavily mobile, frequently USSD or web rather than app, and often served over low-bandwidth connections with feature-phone users in the mix. A deposit-limit interface that assumes a modern smartphone and an authenticated app session is not a safer-gambling control for a meaningful share of these markets. The presence of Lagos State, South Africa, Uganda, Tanzania, Malawi and Kenya's operator association in one campaign also signals the emergence of a pan-African peer group that will start comparing each other's technical standards.",
    "expanded": "LSLGA chief executive Bashir Are said the initiative lets regulators, operators and other stakeholders work together on player protection continent-wide. NLGRB CEO Denis Ngabirano called Uganda's participation an 'important statement of intent', saying the Board's involvement reflects its focus on keeping gaming 'lawful, transparent, entertaining and safe'. The week closes on 11 September with a continental webinar, 'The Hidden Cost of Illegal Gambling: What Does the Black Market Mean for Player Protection?', featuring AiA chief executive Peter Emolemo Kesitilwe, Are, Ngabirano, Gamble Alert CEO Fisayo Oke, pawaTech head of responsible gaming Olabimpe Akingba and NGB acting chief executive Lungile Dukwana. Separately on 7 September the NGB flagged illegal online operators, digital advertising and underage gambling as shared cross-border priorities, with Dukwana saying: 'As gambling markets continue to evolve, player protection must evolve with them.' The choice to close the week on the black market rather than on individual responsibility is notable, and consistent with the pattern in Europe and Brazil of regulators treating unlicensed supply as the primary player-protection failure rather than a separate commercial problem.",
    "source": "https://focusgn.com/africa/africas-first-safer-gambling-week-begins-with-player-protection-focus",
    "tags": ["Africa", "Responsible gambling", "Regulators", "Black market"]
