    "title": "SunBet revenue jumps 35.5% to ZAR 1.18bn as Sun International signals appetite for M&A beyond South Africa",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 8 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Sun International reported SunBet revenue up 35.5% year on year to ZAR 1.18bn, about USD 73.6m, in H1 2026, outpacing 19% growth in South Africa's overall online market. Group income rose 7.4% to ZAR 6.58bn excluding the Table Bay Hotel, now run under an IHG management agreement. On the earnings call CEO Ulrik Bengtsson said the group sees 'plenty of inorganic opportunities', including consolidating minorities and potential SunBet acquisitions 'locally' and 'outside of South Africa', adding: 'we have a very high bar for what we deem to be good investments.' Land-based casino revenue returned to growth for the first time in three years, up 1.5% to ZAR 3.42bn.",
    "why": "The product-mix disclosure is the most useful number here: Bengtsson put SunBet at roughly 90% casino to 10% sportsbook against a market at 'somewhere around 35%-40% sportsbook'. That is a very unusual skew for an African online market, where sports betting typically dominates, and it means SunBet's growth is coming from a different pool than most competitors. It also frames the acquisition logic, since buying sportsbook capability or share is the obvious way to close a 25-30 point mix gap faster than building it. The multi-market platform footprint is equally relevant: SunBet launched in Namibia in May on Bede Gaming's platform, which also powers South Africa and Botswana, with Zambia, Kenya and Ghana under assessment. That is a single-platform, multi-jurisdiction African rollout in progress, and it is a direct competitive reference point for anyone selling multi-market PAM into the region.",
    "expanded": "Bengtsson said: 'We are still open to and keen on consolidating minorities... In terms of the SunBet business, there might be opportunities to consolidate locally, but there might also be opportunities outside of South Africa. We are looking at all of it, but we have a very high bar for what we deem to be good investments.' At its March Capital Markets Day the group targeted doubling SunBet's 4.5% share of the South African online market, though it did not update progress on that goal. SunBet entered Botswana in 2024 and Namibia in May 2026, with Zambia, Kenya and Ghana under assessment. On land-based, South African market share rose 2.3 points to 49% while land-based gross profit dipped 0.7% to ZAR 2bn, so the revenue recovery has not yet translated into margin. The combination of a returning land-based business, a fast-growing online arm with an atypical casino skew, and an explicit statement of cross-border M&A appetite makes Sun International one of the more likely consolidators in sub-Saharan Africa over the next 18 months, and a plausible buyer of either operator share or supplier capability.",
