    "section": "Africa",
    "title": "Botswana Gambling Authority opens an expression-of-interest process to test investor appetite for new bingo and casino licences",
    "meta": "Focus Gaming News Africa | 2 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "The Botswana Gambling Authority has launched an Expression of Interest exercise to gauge investor appetite for developing and operating new bingo and casino establishments, framed as part of its strategic licensing roll-out under the Gambling Act 2012. Submissions close at 3:00pm on 16 October 2026 and must be filed electronically. Interested parties must indicate preferred licence categories and locations drawn from the Authority's approved Licensing and Location Plan, and supply a company profile covering ownership, shareholder information and relevant experience in gambling and adjacent sectors including hospitality, tourism, leisure and property development.",
    "why": "The Authority is explicit that this is not a licence application and confers no entitlement, so its practical value is as a signal of how Botswana intends to structure the eventual Request for Applications, and specifically how heavily local participation will weight the outcome. The requirement to detail Botswana citizen shareholding, board and management representation and local employment suggests localisation obligations will be scored rather than merely stated, which affects corporate structuring long before any technical or platform decision. For suppliers the read is on timing and sequencing: an EOI closing in mid-October, informing licence specifications, then an RFA, puts actual market entry well into 2027, and it means the requirements being shaped now will determine whether cross-border platform provision from a neighbouring licence is viable or whether local presence is mandated. Botswana already has SunBet operating from a 2024 entry, so there is a precedent to reference.",
    "expanded": "Applicants must also set out their development concept, indicative capital expenditure, funding approach and anticipated implementation timeframe, and demonstrate financial and technical capability. The Authority is specifically seeking detail on expected economic impact, employment and citizen participation, including Botswana citizen shareholding, board and management representation and local employment. Participants have been told to keep submissions concise and to exclude protected intellectual property, trade secrets or commercially sensitive material, which is a sensible instruction given that EOI responses may be subject to disclosure. The Authority stressed the EOI is not a licence application or a Request for Applications: participation guarantees no access to any future licensing process and creates no entitlement or expectation that a licence will be granted. Responses will inform licence specifications, application requirements and potential future Requests for Applications. The move follows the regulator's earlier signal that it was progressing to the next phase of bingo and casino licensing, and it lands in the same period as Botswana's parliament passing a gambling bill that retained a proposal to lower the minimum age to 18, so both the commercial perimeter and the player perimeter are being redrawn at once.",
