    "section": "Africa",
    "title": "Ghana's National Lottery Authority plans tiered performance-based support for lotto marketing companies alongside a 5,000-terminal POS rollout",
    "meta": "Focus Gaming News Africa | 7 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "The Ghana National Lottery Authority is developing a performance-based support and reward scheme for Lotto Marketing Companies, under which firms would be categorised as high, medium or low performing based on sales performance. Director general Mohammed Abdul-Salam put the proposal to executives of the Association of Lotto Marketing Companies at a meeting on 2 September that focused on how LMCs could efficiently access the Stabilisation Fund. The NLA said the categorisation 'would help Management develop a structured scheme through which LMCs could be supported or rewarded based on their performance'. The meeting also covered distribution and payment modalities for new Point of Sale terminals under the NLA's Fidelity Bank partnership.",
    "why": "The interesting element is the dependency between the two agenda items. Tiering distributors by sales performance only works if sales performance is measured reliably, and the NLA has previously said it had not procured new machines for 10 years and that weak terminals had hurt sales performance. So the 5,000-terminal Fidelity Bank rollout is the measurement infrastructure on which the incentive scheme depends, and the sequencing risk is obvious if tiers are assigned using data from the equipment being replaced. The growing share of participation via USSD and web adds a further wrinkle, since channel mix differences between distributors will distort any purely POS-derived performance ranking. For anyone supplying retail lottery technology, this is a clear illustration that terminal estate renewal is increasingly bought as a data and settlement capability rather than as hardware.",
    "expanded": "The NLA has not yet detailed the criteria for the three tiers or how support and rewards would be allocated. The proposal builds on talks in May covering the Stabilisation Fund, POS expansion and the growing impact of USSD and web-based lottery participation, at which the NLA said the Fund would be used through consultation and mutual agreement between the Authority, LMCs and the Board. The 2 September meeting also covered modalities for the new Point of Sale terminals under the partnership with Fidelity Bank, specifically their distribution and payment systems. That partnership covers 5,000 POS terminals and was formally signed in July, beginning the rollout phase. The NLA has previously said it had not procured new machines for 10 years and that weak terminals had hurt sales performance, tying the proposed performance scheme to a wider push to modernise sales infrastructure. Ghana's lottery model depends on LMCs as the distribution layer between the Authority and retail agents, so the Stabilisation Fund and the performance tiering together function as the Authority's principal levers over channel behaviour, in a market where digital participation is growing quickly enough to reshape the economics of the physical estate.",
