    "meta": "GGRAsia | 9 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "South Korea's Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism is seeking to amend the Tourism Promotion Act to raise the maximum Tourism Promotion and Development Fund contribution for casino operators from 10% to 15% of annual GGR, and to introduce a five-year licence-renewal system. Industry sources and local outlet Money Today Network say the amendment, originally targeted for September, could slip because of the Chuseok holiday from 24 to 27 September and the National Assembly's parliamentary audit from 6 to 27 October. A ministry official said: 'We are listening to the industry's views and continuing to hold meetings with individual operators.'",
    "why": "The five-year renewal cycle is the more consequential half of this package, and it echoes exactly the concern overseas operators raised about Japan's IR regime in the Hokkaido survey: periodic renewal creates financing uncertainty for capital-intensive assets, because lenders price the risk that a licence is not renewed within the amortisation period. A 15% levy is a margin question that can be modelled; a renewal cliff is a capital-structure question that changes what can be built. Korea Casino Association secretary-general Shin Jong-ho's warning that the higher contribution 'could discourage investment in gaming facilities and expansion' is the predictable industry line, but the ministry weighing transitional arrangements for recently licensed operators suggests it accepts the point in part. The threshold detail matters too, since only revenue above a yet-to-be-set level would attract the 15% band, making the threshold the real negotiation.",
    "expanded": "The ministry official added: 'We originally aimed to propose [the amendment] earlier, but it has been delayed to allow further industry consultation and internal review. We are aiming to submit the amendment later this year.' The ministry is weighing transitional arrangements for operators licensed relatively recently, and says only revenue above a yet-to-be-set threshold would attract the 15% band. Shin Jong-ho, secretary-general of the Korea Casino Association, warned: 'The higher contribution could discourage investment in gaming facilities and expansion.' Representative Cho Gye-won's office said it has not yet received a draft. The Chuseok holiday and the October parliamentary audit together remove most of the available legislative calendar, which makes a late-2026 submission and a 2027 passage the realistic timeline. South Korea's casino sector is split between foreigner-only properties and the single locals-permitted Kangwon Land, and a GGR-linked fund contribution falls hardest on the foreigner-only operators whose recovery has been tied to the return of Chinese and Japanese visitation. The renewal system would be the first time Korean casino licences carried a fixed expiry, replacing what has effectively been indefinite tenure subject to compliance.",
