    "section": "Product",
    "title": "FTC forces Nuvei to pay USD 4.85m and adopt mandatory merchant screening with defined chargeback investigation thresholds",
    "meta": "Federal Trade Commission | 4 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "On 4 September the FTC announced that Nuvei Corporation and subsidiaries Nuvei International Group Limited, Nuvei Limited, SafeCharge Digital Limited and Nuvei Technologies Inc will pay USD 4.85m to settle charges they opened and maintained accounts for merchants they 'knew or should have known were engaged in deception'. The complaint alleges Nuvei processed more than USD 30m in consumer payments for Reimage, an offshore tech-support scam, from 2017 to 2023, via a merchant acquiring bank registered in Cyprus. Charges were brought as unfair practices under the FTC Act plus assisting and facilitating deceptive telemarketers under the Telemarketing Sales Rule.",
    "why": "The order is more useful than the fine because it publishes numeric triggers a US regulator considers adequate merchant risk monitoring, and those numbers now function as a de facto benchmark. Investigation is required where a client's monthly chargeback rate exceeds 1.0% with more than 75 chargebacks in any two of the past six months, or where the total return rate exceeds 2.5% with more than 40 ACH debit returns. Any PSP or platform operating merchant risk programmes should compare its own thresholds against those figures, because divergence is now explainable only deliberately. The explicit prohibition on 'any tactics to avoid fraud or risk monitoring programs established by banks or credit card networks, including load balancing' is the other significant clause, since load balancing across merchant IDs is a widespread practice and the FTC has named it as evasion.",
    "expanded": "US subsidiary Nuvei Technologies allegedly serviced business-opportunity seller DK Automation, tax-authority impersonator American Tax Service, and merchants previously terminated elsewhere for excessive chargebacks or fraud. The proposed order bans Nuvei from providing payment services to any person selling tech support products by telemarketing or by security or performance pop-up messages; prohibits false statements to obtain merchant accounts and any tactics to avoid bank or card-network fraud monitoring including load balancing; and requires screening and monitoring of existing and prospective clients plus 'enhanced screening and investigation of any existing client whose chargeback rates exceed the limits set forth in the order'. The stipulated order, No. 2:26-cv-06306 in the District of Arizona, sets the investigation triggers at a monthly chargeback rate above 1.0% with more than 75 chargebacks in any two of the past six months, or a total return rate above 2.5% with more than 40 ACH debit returns. Christopher Mufarrige, Director of the FTC's Bureau of Consumer Protection, said: 'Consumers deserve a payment system that is competitive, transparent and fortified against fraud.' The Commission vote was 2-0 and the defendants neither admitted nor denied the allegations.",
